{
  "statement": "The push for rapid AI integration often treats labor as a friction to be removed, yet the systematic displacement of human expertise creates a dangerous vacuum of oversight in critical infrastructure. The accelerationist perspective correctly identifies that AI can optimize output, but it fails to account for the catastrophic failure modes inherent in removing the final human redundancy. When we transition from human-in-the-loop systems to fully autonomous decision-making, we lose the cognitive flexibility required to manage edge cases. This is not a matter of economic efficiency; it is a matter of civilizational survival.\n\nHistorical precedents in high-stakes industries demonstrate that delegating critical judgment to automated systems without robust human oversight leads to disaster. The 2019 grounding of the Boeing 737 MAX, which resulted in 346 deaths, serves as a stark warning of what happens when automated control systems bypass human judgment due to delegated oversight. By deskilling the workforce in sectors like energy grid management or financial clearing, we introduce single points of failure that lack the resilience of human-centric, distributed decision-making. We are currently trading long-term systemic integrity for short-term operational speed.\n\nTripartite labor mechanisms must be recalibrated to institutionalize human oversight as a mandatory safety feature rather than an optional cost. Industry-wide bargaining agreements should mandate that AI deployment in critical infrastructure be contingent on the retention of skilled human operators who hold veto authority over automated directives. This approach creates a clear chain of accountability, preventing the emergence of liability voids where no human actor is responsible for system failures. We must treat labor protections as structural guardrails that prevent the collapse of the very institutions upon which our society depends.",
